      "explanation": "<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.885ex\" height=\"1.62ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 833 716\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container><sub>0</sub> of complex [Mo(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>2+</sup> is greater than that of [Cr(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>2+</sup>. It is due to the fact that Mo<sup>2+</sup> belongs to second row transition element while Cr<sup>2+</sup> is first row transition element.</p>\n<p>Magnitude of crystal field splitting energy (<mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.885ex\" height=\"1.62ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 833 716\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container><sub>0</sub>) depends on the charge on the metal ion. Greater the charge, greater is the crystal field splitting energy. Therefore, [Ti(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>3+</sup> &gt; [Ti(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>2+</sup>.</p>"
